// GovernanceActionKind represents what a governance action does to the component, in [RFC-232]'s ChangeSet vocabulary. Both values are declared from the start and only one is produced. That is deliberate: declaring a value and producing it are different things (the mechanism registry carries `iac/bicep` on the same terms), and a client written against this enum today must never meet a value it has not seen — which is exactly what adding one later would cause.
type GovernanceActionKind string

// What a governance action does to the component, in [RFC-232]'s ChangeSet vocabulary. Both values are declared from the start and only one is produced. That is deliberate: declaring a value and producing it are different things (the mechanism registry carries `iac/bicep` on the same terms), and a client written against this enum today must never meet a value it has not seen — which is exactly what adding one later would cause.
const (
	GovernanceActionKindRemoval    GovernanceActionKind = "REMOVAL"    // The component is gone afterwards, so its finding ceases to exist. Every artifact this API produces today is a removal.
	GovernanceActionKindMitigation GovernanceActionKind = "MITIGATION" // The component stays and its risk goes down — a plugin disabled in place, a service stopped. DECLARED, NOT YET PRODUCED: nothing generates one, and an artifact of this kind will not appear until a generator does. See ADR-098's quarantine follow-up.
)

// GovernanceState represents governance (RFC-231): a per-MRN overlay that records whether an inventory entity is sanctioned. **Singular** — an entity carries exactly one of these in a scope. `MANAGED` was removed: "allowed and actively stewarded" is not a governance answer, it is a second question that was riding in the same field.
type GovernanceState string

// Governance (RFC-231): a per-MRN overlay that records whether an inventory entity is sanctioned. **Singular** — an entity carries exactly one of these in a scope. `MANAGED` was removed: "allowed and actively stewarded" is not a governance answer, it is a second question that was riding in the same field.
const (
	GovernanceStateUnclassified GovernanceState = "UNCLASSIFIED" // No decision applies. The default, and never stored — an entity is unclassified precisely when no decision row resolves for it.
	GovernanceStateAllowed      GovernanceState = "ALLOWED"      // Sanctioned. Its presence is expected.
	GovernanceStateOutOfScope   GovernanceState = "OUT_OF_SCOPE" // Deliberately not governed: someone looked and chose not to deal with it. A decision, unlike UNCLASSIFIED, which is the absence of one — this is what stops an entity from coming back for triage.
	GovernanceStateDenied       GovernanceState = "DENIED"       // Not sanctioned; its presence is a violation. Reports it; does not act on it.
	GovernanceStateAutoRemove   GovernanceState = "AUTO_REMOVE"  // Denied **and** standing enforcement: every affected resource is removed whenever governance sees it, with no further decision. The stronger of the two refusing states, and it outranks DENIED at any granularity — a version-level DENIED cannot undercut a product-level AUTO_REMOVE, because that would silently degrade enforcement into a report. Distinct from triggering a one-off removal from DENIED: that acts once on what is there now, this keeps acting on whatever appears later.
)

