// SoftwarePatchState represents what we KNOW about a software install's patch level. The axis a software plan scopes on, and it is three values rather than two because the third is the one that is usually true: **software we hold no newer-version reference for is not up to date, we simply do not know.** Today that is most of a fleet — catalog coverage is partial — so treating unknown as current hides the majority and treating it as a refusal declines the majority. It is neither; it is a bucket a user includes or excludes. Knowing a version is NOT what makes an upgrade possible. Every package manager we generate for takes "bring this package to the newest you have" without being told a version, and the upgrade-available gate asks the endpoint directly rather than comparing against one we supply. So an install in any of these three states can be patched; the state decides whether it is *in the plan*, not whether it *can* be.
type SoftwarePatchState string

// What we KNOW about a software install's patch level. The axis a software plan scopes on, and it is three values rather than two because the third is the one that is usually true: **software we hold no newer-version reference for is not up to date, we simply do not know.** Today that is most of a fleet — catalog coverage is partial — so treating unknown as current hides the majority and treating it as a refusal declines the majority. It is neither; it is a bucket a user includes or excludes. Knowing a version is NOT what makes an upgrade possible. Every package manager we generate for takes "bring this package to the newest you have" without being told a version, and the upgrade-available gate asks the endpoint directly rather than comparing against one we supply. So an install in any of these three states can be patched; the state decides whether it is *in the plan*, not whether it *can* be.
const (
	SoftwarePatchStatePatchAvailable    SoftwarePatchState = "PATCH_AVAILABLE"     // We know a newer version exists: the software catalog's line-latest, or the advisory fixed version, is ahead of what is installed. The upgrade names that version.
	SoftwarePatchStatePatchStateUnknown SoftwarePatchState = "PATCH_STATE_UNKNOWN" // We hold no newer-version reference for this install. Not a claim that it is current and not a claim that it is behind. An upgrade is still offered, versionless — the asset's package manager resolves "newest" itself. The trade is that we cannot afterwards tell whether it moved: `planValidation` reports `UNKNOWN` for these rather than inventing a verdict.
	SoftwarePatchStateUpToDate          SoftwarePatchState = "UP_TO_DATE"          // We hold a reference and the install is at or past it. Worth knowing, and still patchable on request: put it in scope and the change is produced, with the gate making it a no-op if we were right.
)

// SoftwareUpgradeTarget represents which newer version an upgrade moves to. RFC-232 gives a package's fix ladder two candidates at the top, and they are routinely different versions: the highest version that closes the known vulnerabilities, and the latest release the catalog knows — which may sit above it, carrying no additional security fix but being what is actually installable. This is the caller saying which of the two it meant.
type SoftwareUpgradeTarget string

// Which newer version an upgrade moves to. RFC-232 gives a package's fix ladder two candidates at the top, and they are routinely different versions: the highest version that closes the known vulnerabilities, and the latest release the catalog knows — which may sit above it, carrying no additional security fix but being what is actually installable. This is the caller saying which of the two it meant.
const (
	SoftwareUpgradeTargetLatestAvailable SoftwareUpgradeTarget = "LATEST_AVAILABLE" // The software catalog's latest known version within the installed version's release line, never below the advisory fix. Line-scoped rather than globally-latest, so an intentionally-pinned LTS is not told to jump a major. Floored at the advisory fix because the catalog and the vulnerability feed move independently, and a catalog that has not caught up would otherwise make "upgrade to latest" resolve BELOW the version that closes the known CVEs.
	SoftwareUpgradeTargetLatestSecurity  SoftwareUpgradeTarget = "LATEST_SECURITY"  // The advisory fixed version alone — the security update and nothing more. RFC-232's "override down" smaller step, for a fleet that takes security updates only.
)

